# Frozen strict-safe ownership manifests are SHA256-PINNED artifacts.
#
# The apply and rollback scripts hash these files byte-for-byte and refuse to run unless the hash
# equals the reviewed value. That makes them the one class of file in this repository whose bytes
# must survive a checkout unchanged on every platform: git's normal end-of-line normalization would
# hand a Windows clone CRLF and a Linux clone LF, so the same reviewed manifest would hash two
# different ways and the gate could not be satisfied on both.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion while leaving the files diffable and mergeable as text,
# which `binary` would not. The pattern is deliberately narrow — only the generated strict-safe
# ownership manifests, not `reports/` at large.
reports/strict-safe-ownership-batch*/*_manifest.csv   -text
reports/strict-safe-ownership-batch*/*_manifest.json  -text

# The frozen document-filing candidate manifests are pinned the same way — by
# ``document_filing_batch2.CANDIDATE_CSV_SHA256`` — and need the same protection for the same reason.
reports/document-filing-batch*/*_candidate.csv        -text
reports/document-filing-batch*/*_candidate.json       -text

# The D7 Phase C frozen manifest is SHA256-pinned the same way and needs the same protection: it is
# pure LF, core.autocrlf is true, and without this rule a checkout would hand a Windows clone
# CRLF and the reviewed hash could never be satisfied again.
reports/d7_phase_c/*.csv                              -text
reports/d7_phase_c/*.json                             -text

# The D7 machine-attribution Batch 1 manifest is pinned by the same discipline: the apply runner
# will hash the CSV byte-for-byte against the reviewed value, and the sidecar JSON carries that hash
# plus the plan digest. Both must survive a checkout unchanged on every platform.
reports/d7_attribution_batch1/*.csv                   -text
reports/d7_attribution_batch1/*.json                  -text

# The document-verified batch pins four artifacts, and the runner checks all four: the manifest, the
# sidecar that pins it, the summary, and the evidence review the sidecar cites. A checkout that
# changed any of their bytes would make the reviewed hashes unsatisfiable.
reports/d7_document_verified/*.csv                    -text
reports/d7_document_verified/*.json                   -text

# The partial-provenance batch is pinned the same way. Its sidecar additionally names the 229
# documents that authorize the batch, so its bytes carry the evidence itself, not just the plan.
reports/d7_partial_doc/*.csv                          -text
reports/d7_partial_doc/*.json                         -text
